  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q24-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "24",
    "prompt": "Prove that in any list of three consecutive odd numbers, one will be a multiple of 3.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "One of the three numbers is a multiple of 3.\n\nWrite them as $2k+1$, $2k+3$ and $2k+5$, and consider the three possible remainders of $k$ upon division by 3.",
      "worked": "Write the consecutive odd numbers as $2k+1$, $2k+3$ and $2k+5$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$. By the division algorithm, $k$ has one of three forms, with $m\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\nIf $k=3m$, the numbers are\n$$6m+1,\\quad 6m+3,\\quad 6m+5.$$\nThe second number is $6m+3=3(2m+1)$.\n\nIf $k=3m+1$, the numbers are\n$$6m+3,\\quad 6m+5,\\quad 6m+7.$$\nThe first number is $6m+3=3(2m+1)$.\n\nIf $k=3m+2$, the numbers are\n$$6m+5,\\quad 6m+7,\\quad 6m+9.$$\nThe third number is $6m+9=3(2m+3)$.\n\nIn every case, one of the three numbers is a multiple of 3.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 100
  },
